  "full_response": "```ssh\n# General SSH Config File\n\n# Use this config for your personal machine\nHost personal\n  HostName my-personal-vm.example.com\n  User myuser\n  IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_rsa_personal\n  IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_rsaal_encrypted\n  ForwardAgent yes\n  \n# Use this one for your work computer\nHost work\n  HostName work-vm.example.com\n  User workuser\n  IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_rsa_work\n  IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_rsa_work_encrypted\n  ForwardAgent yes\n\n# Encrypted private keys\n# Passphrase for personal key: [Personal Key Passphrase]\n#phrase for work key: [Work Key Passphrase]\n\n# Reference to encrypted files. These are better named according to passwords if possible.\n# The `-i ~/./your-encrypted-identityfile` construction tells ssh and scp to use the \n# specified encrypted file when logging into a remote server.\n```\n\nImportant Note: I strongly discourage putting passphrases within the `.ssh/config` file, as they could be picked up by logging viewing history or being viewed through IDEs. Instead, consider using SSH Key Forwarding or an SSH agent.\n\nAn alternate method can be linking to the decrypted keys residing in a `.perms` file (which does contain a hint towards the encryption pertaining to the files to be encrypted):\n\n```\n# Reference to read-only file with encrypted private keys\nIdentityFilessh/personal-perm\nIdentityFile ~/.ssh/work-perm\n```\n\nThis file should be readable (only for the owner) and contain the passphrases:\n```bash\n# Personal key file\necho -n \"Personal Key Passphrase\" > ~/.ssh/personal-perm\n \n# Work key file\necho -n \"Work Key Passphrase\" > ~/.ssh/work-perm\n```\n\nThe SSH client will ask for the passphrase of the respective key when you log in to a server:\n\n```\nYour personal private key Contents:\nPersonal Key Passphrase is:\n```\n\nI advise searching for alternatives to storing these passphrases, like an HSM or a password manager with envelope encryption, as SSH private keys are one of the top targets of criminals.",
